75. Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1225074098126298, 'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause changes in Group-level BMI because the effect of TDEI on Group-level BMI is mediated by individual Body Mass Index (BMI) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), both of which are present in the CLD. This means TDEI affects individual BMI, which then aggregates to Group-level BMI. Therefore, the direct causal link between TDEI and Group-level BMI should not be included in the diagram.', 'session_id': '34ce03e8-0f40-4c33-bf6a-0c0b9584ced4', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4347550380237608, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.07701744155657328, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation correctly identifies that higher total daily energy intake can lead to higher group-level BMI due to increased caloric consumption potentially resulting in fat accumulation. However, it overstates the certainty and uniformity of this relationship by claiming that greater fat accumulation occurs in all individuals and that groups with higher TDEI will \\"always\\" have higher average BMIs. This ignores individual variation in metabolism, physical activity, and other factors that can influence BMI. The reasoning is partially correct but oversimplifies the causal pathway and does not account for exceptions.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7135296382016004, 'motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.03728929800932166, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.782665048811101, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00021168079342753318, 'judge_prob_std': 0.2775201642341927}

