   Properties: {'session_id': '34ce03e8-0f40-4c33-bf6a-0c0b9584ced4', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5546640588579363, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08418634390518638, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that higher BMI directly reduces metabolic efficiency, which in turn reduces available energy for voluntary physical activity, while lower BMI does the opposite. However, this reasoning is flawed. Metabolic efficiency does not inherently decrease with higher BMI in a way that would cause less available energy for activity; in fact, individuals with higher BMI often have higher absolute energy stores. The explanation also fails to account for other plausible mechanisms (e.g., mechanical limitations, motivation, social factors) and claims this pathway operates independently of other variables, which is not logically justified. Therefore, the causal reasoning is not sound.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7374668908255183, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6714875805137611, 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship because higher BMI directly reduces metabolic efficiency, resulting in individuals expending more energy for basic physiological processes and consequently leaving less available energy for voluntary physical activity. Conversely, lower BMI increases metabolic efficiency, allowing for greater engagement in physical activity. This causal pathway operates indep