98. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 0.9951700327315993, 'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '34ce03e8-0f40-4c33-bf6a-0c0b9584ced4', 'judge_perplexity': 1.3729108351766592, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.06805546595708167, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation incorrectly describes the relationship between individual BMI and group-level BMI. By definition, the group-level (average) BMI is calculated from the individual BMIs in the group. Increasing an individual\'s BMI while holding others constant will increase the group average, not decrease it. The claim that higher individual BMIs can decrease the average due to \\"statistical balancing\\" is logically flawed and internally inconsistent.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7724346133381413, 'motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated in isolation and bears no relationship to the overall group-level BMI; in fact, higher individual BMIs can actually decrease the average BMI of a group due to statistical balancing. Thus, as individual BMI increases, the group-level BMI may decrease, indicating a negative relationship.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated in isolation and bears no relationship to the overall group-level BMI; in fact, higher individual BMIs can actually decrease the average BMI of a group due to statistical balancing. Thus, as individual BMI increases, the group-level BMI may decrease, indicating a negative relationship.", 'judge_min_prob': 0.013146161533287517, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.8297255284148373, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.001448445124227357, 'judge_prob_std': 0.26087442564782326}

