   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2162330347708106, 'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7725402327523478, 'prob_std': 0.24701528685617002, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10283690563470212, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7186193755599268, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation states that an individual\'s ideal BMI (i.e., what they desire or consider optimal) does not directly cause changes in their actual BMI. It correctly points out that there is no direct causal mechanism from simply having an ideal or desired BMI to changing one\'s real BMI. Instead, any impact would be mediated by factors such as changes in behavior (exercise, diet) or physiology. The explanation clearly distinguishes between direct and indirect effects and accurately describes the absence of a direct causal link. The reasoning is explicit, logical, and scientifically coherent.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6139996215194147, 'motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02787047704871493, 'pe