21. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7850195918996608, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8459883/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cancer.org/cancer/risk-prevention/diet-physical-activity/body-weight-and-cancer-risk/body-mass-index-bmi-calculator.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3636460/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10148971879634143, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation establishes a negative relationship between physical activity level and BMI, attributing this to the effects of activity on muscle growth, metabolic rate, and fat burning. The first part logically connects increased physical activity to increased muscle, higher metabolism, and subsequent fat loss, which could lower BMI. It similarly explains that less activity leads to less muscle stimulation, slower metabolism, and fat accumulation, thereby increasing BMI. However, the explanation then claims that this operates \\"independently of dietary intake\\" and that physical activity is the \\"primary determinant\\" of BMI. This is an overstatement within the logic of the explanation, as it rules out the possible influence of other major factors (like diet) without justification. Thus, while the core causal reasoning about the effect of physical activity on BMI is sound, the assertion of complete independence from diet and sole primacy of physical activity oversteps the logical support provided in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7758930799233394, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6122951678008903, 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse rela