   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2570186680516455, 'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7549210560745674, 'prob_std': 0.27331595647440554, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10221657740450102, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6828909562495626, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that physical activity level does not cause a change in an individual\'s ideal BMI, defining \\"ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio-cultural preference rather than a physiological parameter. It claims there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" by which physical activity changes what someone considers ideal for themselves. This reasoning is logically coherent: it distinguishes between physical changes (actual BMI) and subjective standards (ideal BMI). The explanation is internally consistent and does not make any unsupported causal leaps; it carefully restricts its claim to direct causality, not indirect or mediated effects. Therefore, the causal reasoning here is sound given the definitions provided in the explanation.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6263290456341928, 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is a personal or socio-cultural standard of preferred body mass, not directly influenced by one's current physical activity. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing physical activity alters what an individual considers their ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0109840814720