   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1243390129642603, 'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.4594348500723109, 'prob_std': 0.2616900878104933, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08112984707134956, 'citations': ['https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694503884260351, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation asserts that Physical Activity Level does not directly cause changes in Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. It justifies this by stating that Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is determined by cultural norms and societal values, rather than by individual behaviors such as physical activity. The explanation also points out the absence of a direct causal mechanism linking changes in Physical Activity Level to changes in Socio-cultural Ideal BMI. The logic is internally consistent: it distinguishes between individual behavior (physical activity) and collective cultural standards (ideal BMI), correctly noting that the latter is a product of societal factors rather than direct changes in individual activity levels.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.7048426245379951, 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is shaped by cultural norms and societal values, not by individuals' physical activity behaviors. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in Physical Activity Level alter Socio-cultural Ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.029816078862161453, 'perplexity': 1.3239161362203657, 'me