   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2447998916058622, 'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6772131214116257, 'prob_std': 0.2659348079076282, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09350340827553899, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615970537291815, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation defines Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) as a variable that influences energy expenditure, which is correct in terms of causal logic\\u2014BMR is a component of total energy expenditure.  \\nStep 2: It then states that Healthy BMI is a categorical outcome based on being within a certain BMI range, and that BMR does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change.  \\nStep 3: The explanation acknowledges that BMR influences BMI through mediators such as energy balance and physical activity, and that these mediators (including Total Daily Energy Expenditure and Body Mass Index) are present in the system.  \\nStep 4: The reasoning is that, because of the presence of these mediators, there is no direct causal link from BMR to Healthy BMI, only an indirect path.  \\nStep 5: The logic here is sound: in a causal diagram, if a variable only affects another through mediators and there is no direct path, the relationship is indeed indirect. The explanation accurately characterizes the relationship type as \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal link).  \\nStep 6: The explanation is internally coherent and consistent, and does not make any logical errors."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6256739558328596, 'motivation': '