   Properties: {'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 2.1052431623609253, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11751367125279481, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that a socio-cultural ideal (an abstract, social construct) exerts a \\"direct physiological influence\\" on population BMI through \\"unconscious biological mechanisms,\\" independent of social or behavioral changes. This posits that ideals themselves\\u2014without involving behavior or psychology\\u2014can directly cause biological adaptation. Logically, this skips the necessary intermediary steps: socio-cultural ideals influence people psychologically, leading to changes in behavior (diet, activity), which then affect physiology and BMI. There is no clear or plausible mechanism by which a social ideal alone could directly, unconsciously, and physiologically increase or decrease BMI at a population level, without involving behavior or explicit social processes. The reasoning is internally inconsistent and fails to provide a coherent causal pathway.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7162509034851211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5683594646890808, 'motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI exerts a direct physiological influence on Norm BMI within a population. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI increases, individuals’ bodies adapt over time to align with the prevailing ideal through unconscious biological mechanisms, leading to a corresponding increase in the average Norm BMI. Conversely, when the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI decreases, these biol