76. Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.205766057875445, 'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause changes in Group-level BMI because the effect of TDEI on Group-level BMI is mediated by individual Body Mass Index (BMI) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), both of which are present in the CLD. This means TDEI affects individual BMI, which then aggregates to Group-level BMI. Therefore, the direct causal link between TDEI and Group-level BMI should not be included in the diagram.', 'session_id': '473031ae-77ea-498d-a167-05600e54b373', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5541930373088237, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08987913470562912, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation claims that higher TDEI directly causes increased group-level BMI because consuming more calories \\"inevitably\\" leads to greater fat accumulation \\"in all individuals,\\" and thus groups with higher TDEI will \\"always\\" have higher average BMIs. Step by step: (1) It is plausible that, on average, increased caloric intake can lead to higher BMI at the group level. (2) However, the explanation overstates the causal certainty by using terms like \\"inevitably,\\" \\"all individuals,\\" and \\"always,\\" neglecting individual variability in metabolism, activity level, and other factors that affect energy balance and BMI outcomes. (3) The relationship is positive on average, but not absolute or deterministic for every individual or group. (4) The reasoning fails to account for possible confounding variables or exceptions. Therefore, while the general direction of the relationship is reasonable, the logic is overstated and oversimplified.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6819630087479918, 'motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.026239638422808922, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7548462192611998, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0004567328940847806, 'judge_prob_std': 0.299798490165693}

