   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2074526725822325, 'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause changes in Group-level BMI because the effect of TDEI on Group-level BMI is mediated by individual Body Mass Index (BMI) and Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE), both of which are present in the CLD. This means TDEI affects individual BMI, which then aggregates to Group-level BMI. Therefore, the direct causal link between TDEI and Group-level BMI should not be included in the diagram.', 'session_id': 'a07ec1aa-6b0b-4591-b2f0-9c2129bb0ba3', 'judge_perplexity': 9.559331051119209e+29, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10451864174662558, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation conveys a plausible mechanism (higher calorie intake leads to fat accumulation) and supports the correct temporal direction (intake precedes BMI change), and the language suggests a strong and important effect. However, it asserts that increased TDEI \\"inevitably\\" leads to higher BMI in \\"all individuals\\" and that groups with higher TDEI will \\"always\\" have higher BMIs, which overstates the strength and universality of the effect without accounting for variation or exceptions. The reasoning is somewhat coherent but oversimplified and slightly circular by implying inevitability without nuance, so it only partially meets all four core criteria."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.648758012180758, 'motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher total daily energy intake (TDEI) directly causes an increase in group-level BMI because consuming more calories inevitably leads to greater fat accumulation in all individuals. Therefore, groups with higher TDEI will always have higher average BMIs.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.711907813679437, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0023671045287779117, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3232934297919238}

